      "username": "phantaskippy",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/phantaskippy/{size}/1897_2.png",
      "created_at": "2014-09-17T13:48:38.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"xThe Killswitch\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n xThe Killswitch:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Whenever a card affects a hero's hand or trash, the card refers to \"a hero.\" Since The Sentinels isn't the first deck to deal with multiple targets (Unity's droids, Expatriette's flak jacket, Visionary's projection), I believe there has been no problem with affecting an entire hero's board as far as taking damage. It's always been referred to as hero targets.So yeah, I fully understand what a hero TARGET is. I just wasn't sure if each Sentinel in that deck counts toward \"H,\" since they can each be incapacitated, each have a power, etc.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>They are only incapacitated when their instruction card is flipped. &nbsp;All of the Sentinels have flip side artwork, but those are not incap sides because there are no incap abilities on them. &nbsp;All four have to be flipped, then their character card flips that has the incap abilities, then the Sentinels are incapped.</p>",
