    {
      "id": 118299,
      "name": "Ronway",
      "username": "Ronway",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ronway/{size}/1856_2.png",
      "created_at": "2014-09-17T13:56:22.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"phantaskippy\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/phantaskippy/48/1897_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> phantaskippy:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<div><b>xThe Killswitch wrote:</b><br><blockquote>Whenever a card affects a hero's hand or trash, the card refers to \"a hero.\" Since The Sentinels isn't the first deck to deal with multiple targets (Unity's droids, Expatriette's flak jacket, Visionary's projection), I believe there has been no problem with affecting an entire hero's board as far as taking damage. It's always been referred to as hero targets.So yeah, I fully understand what a hero TARGET is. I just wasn't sure if each Sentinel in that deck counts toward \"H,\" since they can each be incapacitated, each have a power, etc.\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote></div>\nThey are only incapacitated when their instruction card is flipped. &nbsp;All of the Sentinels have flip side artwork, but those are not incap sides because there are no incap abilities on them. &nbsp;All four have to be flipped, then their character card flips that has the incap abilities, then the Sentinels are incapped.\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>The back of each individual member of The Sentinels are incapacitated sides, it even so says on the back of the card. The rulebook also says that the setup card expains what happens when all 4 members of the The Sentinels are incapped. So each member of The Sentinels have an incap side, but The Sentinels are not incapped until all 4 are incapped.</p>",
